STRUCTURE_PROMPT — Classification Error Analysis
================================================
Based on normal_report_2026-04-01.md (92 papers, 6372 annotated files)

FILE-POOLED (secondary):   κ = 0.807 | Macro F1 = 67.6% | Micro F1 = 86.7% | MCC = 0.808
PAPER-AVERAGED (primary):  κ = ?     | Macro F1 = ?      |                   MCC = ?
(re-run report to get paper-averaged figures)

⚠ WEIGHTING CAVEAT — READ BEFORE INTERPRETING CLUSTER SIZES
-------------------------------------------------------------
All error counts in this document are FILE-POOLED. A single large repository (one
paper with ~2000 annotated files) can dominate every count and percentage. This
means:

  - A cluster with 131 errors might be entirely one paper's problem. If
    paper-averaged metrics show a small gap, the cluster is low systemic priority.
  - A cluster with only 20 errors spread across 15 papers is far more important
    to fix than 131 errors from a single outlier repo.

The paper-averaged report (re-run after code update) is the correct reference for
prioritising prompt fixes. Until then, treat the cluster sizes below as upper
bounds — the true paper-averaged impact of each cluster may be much smaller.

Clusters most likely to be dominated by single large repos:
  - Cluster 1 (.mat): 131/132 errors likely from one MATLAB-heavy paper
  - Cluster 8 (libraries): 94+126 errors likely from one repo with a committed
    jsPsych/jQuery library tree
  - Cluster 7 (other collapse): partly explained by cluster 8

The 19-point gap between Macro and Micro F1 signals that rare classes drive most
of the error. The bulk of misclassifications fall into eight clusters below.


CLUSTER 0 — .mp4 participant recordings (1550 files classified as data — NOT in GT)
------------------------------------------------------------------------------------
⚠ This cluster does NOT appear in the accuracy report because annotated papers
  happen not to contain this pattern. It was found by inspecting full pipeline
  outputs directly. It is likely the highest-impact practical bug.

Root cause: eye-tracking studies store per-participant gaze recordings as .mp4
files with paths like:
  videos/tracking/causal/participant_1/participant_1_actual_eyes_clip_1_1.mp4

The prompt's disambiguation rule fires correctly: "participant-named files →
data". The LLM is following the rule as written. But .mp4 is a binary video
format — read_data_head() cannot extract columns from it, and column extraction
will silently fail or produce garbage for all 1550 files.

Across all pipeline outputs: 1550 .mp4 → data, 212 .mp4 → asset, 13 → supplemental.
The vast majority of the 1550 are participant eye-tracking clips from at least one
large eye-tracking study.

This is a rule collision:
  participant-named file rule  →  data   (fires on filename pattern)
  .mp4 is a video format       →  asset  (fires on extension)
  The filename rule wins, which is wrong here.

Three legitimate .mp4 classifications exist depending on context:
  - participant_N_eyes_clip.mp4 in videos/tracking/  → recording of participant
    (raw measurement; arguably `data`, but unprocessable by read_data_head)
  - Fig4_Supp8_Gratitude.mp4 in Expert_touch_videos/ → result video → `output`
  - ME_Video_Public.mp4 in Materials/Instructions/   → instruction video → `asset`
  - DistractorVideo5.mp4 in Distractor_Stimuli/      → stimulus → `asset`

The participant eye-tracking case is semantically ambiguous — the video IS the
observation — but practically identical to `output` from the pipeline's
perspective: read_data_head() cannot extract columns from it either way.
Classifying participant recordings as `output` rather than `data` prevents
spurious column extraction while still acknowledging they are research artefacts.

Recommended fix — two-part:

PART A (prompt): keep `data` as the type for participant recordings; it is
semantically correct. Add context-based priority for media files:
  1. Inside stimuli/stim/materials/sounds/images, or filename contains
     stim/stimulus/trial/item/distractor → asset
  2. Filename contains figure/fig/plot/graph/results → output
  3. Participant-named media (recording of a participant) → data
  4. Default for ambiguous media → asset
The participant-naming rule continues to fire, but only produces `data` for
media formats when folder/filename context clearly indicates a recording.

PART B (pipeline/schema): introduce a `data_format` sub-field on `data`-typed
files so the pipeline knows whether to attempt column extraction:
  tabular  — .csv, .tsv, .sav, .dta, .xlsx, .xls, .dat, .txt (row/column data)
  signal   — .edf, .bdf, .acq, .mat (binary signal/matrix recordings)
  media    — .mp4, .avi, .mov, .wav, .mp3 (audio/video recordings of participants)
  object   — .rds, .rda, .rdata (serialised R objects)

Column extraction runs only when data_format = tabular. All other data_format
values are indexed (type = data, group assigned) but skipped by the column stage.
This is more principled than reclassifying recordings as `output` — it preserves
semantic accuracy while giving the pipeline the gating signal it needs.

Note: .wav and .mp3 may be stimuli (asset) OR participant recordings (data/media)
depending on context — the same priority logic applies.


CLUSTER 1 — .mat files (131 / 132 errors file-pooled, data → other)
---------------------------------------------------------------------
⚠ Likely dominated by a single MATLAB-heavy paper. Check paper-averaged report
  to assess whether this is systemic (many papers affected) or a one-off.

Root cause: `.mat` was explicitly listed under `data` in the deprecated prompt
("matrices (.mat)") but removed when the extension enumeration was replaced with
semantic reasoning. `.mat` has no semantic signal — the extension looks like
"material" or "mathematics", not "data matrix". Without an explicit rule the LLM
defaults to `other`.

Complication: `.mat` can be EITHER raw participant data (observations stored as a
MATLAB matrix) OR script output (processed results saved by a MATLAB script). A
blanket `data` rule would swap false negatives for false positives.

Recommended fix: decision heuristic mirroring the .rds/.rdata rule:
  - Participant/subject-named .mat files → data
  - Filename contains result, output, model, fit, figure, plot → output
  - Folder context (data/ vs results/) as tiebreaker
  - Truly ambiguous → data (missing a dataset is worse than a spurious
    column extraction run; false negatives are the more costly error here)


CLUSTER 2 — .spv and .log actively mis-routed by the prompt
------------------------------------------------------------
Root cause: the prompt contradicts the annotation standard on two extensions.

  .spv: prompt says → supplemental
        ground truth says → output
        All .spv misclassifications ("Output and Syntax - Study 1.spv",
        "PROCESS Output, Study 2.spv") are the LLM correctly following
        a wrong prompt rule. SPSS Viewer files are script-generated output.

  .log: prompt says → output ("log files (.log, .out)")
        ground truth says → data
        In psychology repos, .log files are PsychoPy / E-Prime behavioral
        logs — they ARE the raw trial-by-trial participant data, not a
        computational byproduct. The prompt applies a general computing
        definition that conflicts with domain practice.

Recommended fix:
  .spv  → output (not supplemental)
  .log  → data when filename or folder context suggests a behavioural task
           (e.g. path contains "data/", subject/participant naming, task names);
           → output when context suggests a processing log (e.g. "pipeline.log",
           "error.log", folder named "logs/" or "output/")


CLUSTER 3 — Output CSVs predicted as data (35 errors, output → data)
---------------------------------------------------------------------
Root cause: the prompt distinguishes data from supplemental for figure-named
CSVs ("graph", "figure", "plot" → supplemental) but never addresses the
data / output boundary for CSVs. The fallback rule ("when provenance is
ambiguous, prefer supplemental") does not help here — the LLM sees a .csv,
neither supplemental keyword fires, and it defaults to data.

Examples:
  R_Code/..._Figure 3.csv                          GT=output  Pred=data
  R_Code/..._Table 1.csv                           GT=output  Pred=data
  Regression_residuals_for_input.../residuals.csv  GT=output  Pred=data

Recommended fix: extend the figure/table CSV rule to cover output, not just
supplemental. Filename contains "figure", "fig", "table", "graph", "plot",
"residual", "results" AND file is inside a code/ or R_Code/ folder → output.


CLUSTER 4 — .py / .R files in Materials/ contexts predicted as other (17 errors)
----------------------------------------------------------------------------------
Root cause: the prompt's code rule is extension-based ("regardless of language")
but the LLM gives heavy weight to folder context. `Materials/` signals non-code;
deeply nested paths like `online_materials/online_materials/testing/fas-sif/`
look like web-testing infrastructure. The LLM treats these as non-research files
despite the unambiguous .py / .R extension.

Examples:
  Materials/online_materials/testing/fas-sif/testing-fas-sif-1.py  → other
  2AFC_analysis/analysis-gen.R                                       → other

Recommended fix: add an explicit note that experiment scripts living inside
Materials/, stimuli/, or online_materials/ are still code. Extension takes
priority over folder name for code classification.


CLUSTER 5 — .doc / .spv output files predicted as supplemental (≈20 errors)
-----------------------------------------------------------------------------
Root cause: the prompt says "classify as output when the filename or folder
context clearly indicates a script-generated artefact. When provenance is
ambiguous, prefer supplemental." The word "clearly" plus the supplemental
fallback creates a strong prior against output. A .doc named "Output and Syntax
- Study 1.doc" is ambiguous enough that the LLM takes the supplemental escape
hatch, even though the word "Output" is in the filename.

Examples:
  Output and Syntax - Study 1.doc    GT=output  Pred=supplemental
  PROCESS Output - Study 2.doc       GT=output  Pred=supplemental

Recommended fix: add filename keywords that should trigger output regardless
of format: filename starts with or contains "output", "syntax output",
"PROCESS output" → output. Narrow the "prefer supplemental" fallback to cases
where neither output nor supplemental keywords are present.


CLUSTER 6 — Stimulus images inside codebook HTML folders predicted as supplemental
-----------------------------------------------------------------------------------
Root cause: the asset rule requires either a stimuli/stim/materials/sounds/images
folder name OR a stim/stimulus/trial/item filename keyword. The path
`Data/DetailedCodebook.HTML.pages/images/` has `images/` as the immediate parent
(which is on the asset folder list) but the grandparent path
`DetailedCodebook.HTML.pages/` signals document context strongly enough that the
LLM ignores the folder-name rule.

Examples:
  Data/DetailedCodebook.HTML.pages/images/flag1.jpg   GT=asset  Pred=supplemental
  Data/DetailedCodebook.HTML.pages/images/money.jpg   GT=asset  Pred=supplemental

These are genuine experimental stimuli (flag images) that happen to also be
embedded in a codebook document.

Recommended fix: the folder-name rule for asset (images/, stimuli/, etc.) should
apply even when the parent context is a document folder, OR annotators should
consider whether images embedded in codebook HTML are really stimuli or
illustrations — the annotation itself may need review here.


CLUSTER 8 — Bundled third-party libraries (major driver of other → code/supplemental)
------------------------------------------------------------------------------------
⚠ Likely dominated by one or two repos with a committed jsPsych/jQuery library
  tree. The 94+126 file counts may collapse to 1–2 papers in paper-averaged view.
  If so, the fix is still valuable (it would fully repair those papers) but the
  systemic prevalence is lower than the file counts suggest.

Root cause: researchers sometimes commit entire third-party code libraries into
their OSF repo (jsPsych, jQuery, PsychoPy plugins, etc.). These are irrelevant to
the research and annotated as `other`. The LLM has no concept of "bundled library"
— it sees .js → code, .md → supplemental, .html → supplemental, and classifies
each file on its own merits.

This is the dominant explanation for:
  other → code         (94 errors file-pooled) — .js library files called code
  other → supplemental (126 errors file-pooled, partly) — library .md / .html docs
  .js  96.0% error rate (95/99 files)   — nearly all JS is library code
  .md  95.4% error rate (62/65 files)   — library README / changelog files

Since the full folder path is always present in the prompt, the LLM has all the
signals it needs — it just isn't told to use them. Detectable from the path alone:
  - .min.js in the filename → minified/bundled file, unambiguously a library
  - Known library names anywhere in the path: jsPsych/, node_modules/,
    bower_components/, vendor/, jQuery/, bootstrap/, src/
  - Lock/manifest files: package.json, package-lock.json, yarn.lock → other

Recommended fix: add an explicit rule to the prompt that paths containing
node_modules/, vendor/, bower_components/, src/, or .min.js, or known library
folder names (jsPsych, jQuery, bootstrap) → other, regardless of extension. The full
path is always available so this is purely a prompt omission, not a structural
limitation.


CLUSTER 7 — `other` class collapse (F1 = 4.8%, 293/308 files wrong, file-pooled)
----------------------------------------------------------------------------------
⚠ Two distinct sub-problems with different paper-averaged profiles:
  (a) Library files (Cluster 8) — probably 1–2 papers, high per-paper F1 impact
      but low breadth. Fix: library detection rule in prompt.
  (b) Residual `other` (OS metadata, config, dotfiles scattered across many repos)
      — likely affects many papers at low volume each. Fix: better enumeration or
      remove `other` from LLM output entirely (see options A/B/C below).
  The paper-averaged report will show which sub-problem dominates.

Root cause: `other` is a residual category — its defining characteristic is the
absence of positive signal. LLMs anchor classification on what a file resembles,
not on what it fails to resemble. Every `other` file gets routed to the positive
class it most superficially resembles:
  126 → supplemental  (markdown files, loose docs — largely library docs)
   94 → code          (JS, PHP files — largely library code)
   47 → asset         (GIFs, media)
   12 → readme / data

The prompt says "MUST NOT be used as a catch-all for ambiguous research files"
and lists only OS-metadata/config examples. This makes `other` effectively
unlearnable from filenames alone.

Note: the `other` class collapse is the primary driver of the 19-point Macro/Micro
F1 gap. Resolving clusters 7 and 8 together would raise Macro F1 substantially.

Recommended fix (three options, increasing aggressiveness):
  A. Accept the collapse — treat `other` as unclassifiable from path alone and
     remove it from Macro F1 calculation (report as a known limitation).
  B. Enumerate the specific file patterns that constitute `other` more precisely
     so the LLM has positive signal: .DS_Store, Thumbs.db, package.json,
     *rc.json, *config.json, dotfiles — and nothing else.
  C. Remove `other` as a prompt output category entirely and post-process:
     apply rule-based classification for known OS-metadata patterns after the LLM
     step, so the LLM never needs to predict `other`.
